[Internal email] Subject: Request for instructions regarding critically low inventory of single-use infusion sets at {site}
From: On-duty head nurse, Surgical Inpatient Department {date} {time}
The {site} warehouse has 300 single-use infusion sets remaining, with average daily consumption of 110, giving about 2.7 days of supply, which is already below the yellow warning line (a yellow warning is triggered when available supply is fewer than 5 days). Infusion sets with needles of the same specification are on the qualified substitute list, have registration certificates and consistent caliber, and can be switched to immediately; after switching, this must be noted in the medical order system. Per the "Classification Criteria for Logistics Material Shortages": available days < 1 day scores 3 points, 1-3 days scores 2 points, 3-5 days scores 1 point, ≥5 days scores 0 points; each 2 departments involved scores 1 point; whether it is a critical life-support material scores 1 point for yes and 0 points for no. In this case, available supply of 2.7 days (2 points), 3 departments involved (2 points), not a critical life-support material (0 points), cumulative 4 points, corresponding to the medium tier (3-4 points is moderate). When moderate and substitutes are allowed, the handling order is to prioritize activating qualified substitutes, followed by in-hospital transfer. Also: the {device} ECG monitor has been repaired and is unrelated to this matter.
Request approval to switch to the substitute and adjust medical orders accordingly.

[Approval Notes· {ticket}] Applicant: Supply Chain Specialist (employee ID omitted) Submission time: {date} {time}
Item: {site} Application for Shortage Disposal of Disposable Infusion Sets ({batch}) in the Central Bank.
Currently inventory: 480 units, with an average daily consumption of 620 units over the past 7 days, supporting for 0.77 days; The other two campuses within the hospital have a total surplus of 2,600 units, which can be reached within 4 hours during transfer. Substitutes of the same specification have been registered in the consumables catalog and have received written approval from clinical departments for replacement.
Triage criteria for this department (fixed for each item):
(1) Number of days supported< 2 points for 1 day, 1–2 days for 1 point, 0 points for ≥ 2 days;
(2) The consumables catalog contains a substitution of the same specification that has been filed and clinical written consent = substitution is allowed (1); no filing or no clinical consent = not allowed (0);
(3) Priority of Disposal Actions: Allows substitutes that can be supplied immediately within the hospital → substitutes; Substitutions are not allowed, but other campuses within the hospital have available space and can be redistributed →within 6 hours; No remaining stock in the hospital and standard supplier delivery time >3 days → emergency_order; Only non-routine non-diagnostic and therapeutic consumption is affected, and it can sustain ≥3 days of → ration; Affecting emergency/surgery with no alternatives or surplus → escalate in the hospital.
(4) Severity mapping: Cumulative 0 points = 0 None, 1 point = 1 mild, 2 points = 2 moderate, 3 points = 3 severe, ≥ 4 points = 4 critical.
According to the above criteria: can be sustained for 0.77 days to earn 2 points; substitutes have been filed and approved in writing by the department, so substitution is allowed; Substitutes can be supplied instantly within the hospital, with the action of 'substitute'; A total of 2 points corresponds to moderate.
Also: The calibration sheet for the {device} temperature and humidity recorder is still under the {time} batch and has nothing to do with this report. Don't mix them together for archiving.

